@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia — running the protocol on the harms a protection names
#
# The statement at generalizations/the-protection-and-what-would-show-it-failed.ttl
# was written on 2026-09-20 and named its own first corpus in the same breath:
# the Mesopotamian apotropaic literature, on the reasoning that a manual
# written for practitioners rather than for buyers has a reason to say in
# advance what the protection does not cover. This file records what happened
# when that was done on the same day, which is the whole point of writing a
# falsifier down.
#

pmp:ApotropaicHarmListSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether an apotropaic corpus sorts the harms it names"@en, "Ob ein apotropäisches Korpus die Schäden sortiert, die es nennt"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "R. Campbell Thompson, The Devils and Evil Spirits of Babylonia, being Babylonian and Assyrian incantations against the demons, ghouls, vampires, hobgoblins, ghosts and kindred evil spirits which attack mankind, Luzac's Semitic Text and Translation Series vols. XIV and XV (London: Luzac, 1903 and 1904), both volumes complete: the Utukki Limnuti series in volume I and the Fever Sickness and Headache series in volume II, with their introductions" ;
    pm:searchedFor "The falsifier stated at pmc:TheProtectionAndWhatWouldShowItFailedExamination, in the corpus that protocol names as the place to run it first. Three things would have failed the statement: an operative text that marks off the harms whose occurrence a bystander could establish from those whose occurrence nobody could record; one that names a recordable harm as the test of whether the protection held; or one that states a condition under which the protection is to be judged to have failed." ;
    pm:searchTerms "Whole-text search of both volumes on the whitespace-normalised text. For a term or a sign: shall recover, shall die, shall live, shall be healed, be cured, not be cured, thou shalt know, seventh day, third day, if he, unless. For the protection formulae themselves: come not nigh, draw not nigh, may they not enter, get thee, shall not approach, be thou removed. Every hit of every pattern was read in context. The recurring litany was located by its own wording and counted."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:AsipuLitaniaMalorum, pmc:AsipuNominisIncertitudo, pmc:AsipuUtukkiYieldsRemotio ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-20" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-20" ;
    skos:note "No falsifier. Neither volume contains, in any operative text, a sign by which a protection would be known to have held, a term within which the harm would otherwise have come, or a condition under which the adjuration is to be judged to have failed. The search was designed to find one: the second volume is titled for fever, sickness and headache, and Babylonian diagnostic literature elsewhere is full of he shall recover and he shall die, so a prognosis attached to an incantation was the likely refutation. There is none in either volume. Every hit for shall recover and shall die was zero; the three hits for seventh day and the three for if he and unless all fall in the editor's introduction or in the hemerology of unlucky days, which is a calendar and not a test of a protection."@en ;
    skos:note "A confirming case instead, and a sharper one than the statement had. The fixed litany of the Utukki Limnuti mixes the two kinds in the enumeration of what is adjured — pestilence, fever, sickness, death and disease standing in one list with spirit, demon, ghost, devil, god, fiend, Phantom of Night and Wraith of Night — and again in the enumeration of what is asked to be kept off: unto my body may they not draw nigh, before me may they wreak no evil, nor follow behind me, into my house may they not enter, my fence may they not break through, into my chamber may they not enter. A fence broken through is among the plainest facts any witness in this holding names, and it is set beside a wreaking of evil in the abstract with nothing to separate them."@en ;
    skos:note "One thing the search found that the statement did not anticipate. Tablet III does not list what is present but disjoins what might be — whether thou be an evil Spirit or an evil Demon, or sickness, or death, or evil pestilence — so the operation is written not to require that the officiant establish which harm he is treating. That is a second and independent way of making the claim unfailable, and it is not the mixing of kinds; it is a refusal to identify the kind at all. It is held at pmc:AsipuNominisIncertitudo and is a reason to widen the protocol rather than to narrow the statement."@en ;
    skos:note "Limits of this run, and they are the ordinary ones. One edition, and an old one: Campbell Thompson's readings of 1903 and 1904 are superseded almost everywhere, and the holding's other Mesopotamian nodes cite Geller's critical edition, which this run could not obtain. What the search establishes is a fact about an enumeration and not about a verb, and an enumeration is the part of a text that survives a bad translation best. The namburbi rituals proper, which the protocol also named, are edited in a copyrighted work and were not reached; they remain the strongest place for a second run."@en .
